ef="IJBI2009-506120.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>					<italic>&#x003b2;</italic>  variation inhorizontal subbands of a set of scintigraphic images  (in black the polynomial  <italic>y</italic>that approximates the average variation).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Comparison of the method with several othermethods, on a bone scintigraphic image.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Comparison of the method with several othermethods, on a heart scintigraphic image.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Example of denoising images acquired by ascendanttime.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Example of a slide ofcomparative tests.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Example of a slide of forcedtests.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJBI2009-506120.008"/></fig><table-wrap id="tab1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Statistics on the value of <italic>&#x003b2;</italic> (for 100 scintigraphic images).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>&#x003b2;</italic> &#x02264; 0.5</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.5 &#x02264; <italic>&#x003b2;</italic> &#x0227a; 1</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1 &#x02264; <italic>&#x003b2;</italic> &#x0227a; 1.5</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.5 &#x02264; <italic>&#x003b2;</italic> &#x0227a; 2</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>&#x003b2;</italic> &#x0227b; 2</th></tr></thead><tbody>